# Chapter list: 6th edition, from the publisher's table of contents (us.elsevierhealth.com).
# Summaries cover each chapter's standard topics; they are written for this site, not copied from the book.

def ch(n, title, text, keys):
    return (n, title, [text], keys)

C = [
    ch(1, "Introductory Questions", "Occupational therapy (OT) helps people of all ages participate in the everyday activities ('occupations') they need and want to do, such as self-care, work, school, play, and leisure. OT practitioners include occupational therapists (OTs, who need a master's or doctoral degree) and occupational therapy assistants (OTAs, who need an associate degree and work under OT supervision). Both must pass the NBCOT exam and meet state licensure rules.", ["Occupation = meaningful everyday activity", "OT: master's or doctorate; OTA: associate", "NBCOT certification exam", "OTAs work under OT supervision"]),
    ch(2, "Looking Back: A History of Occupational Therapy", "OT grew out of the moral treatment movement and the Arts and Crafts movement. The profession was founded in 1917 by founders including George Barton, William Rush Dunton Jr., Eleanor Clarke Slagle, and Susan Tracy; Slagle developed habit training. OT expanded to help soldiers after the World Wars and shifted between occupation-based and medical-model approaches over time.", ["Founded in 1917", "Eleanor Clarke Slagle: habit training", "Moral treatment movement roots", "Growth after the World Wars"]),
    ch(3, "Philosophical Base and Values of Occupational Therapy", "OT's philosophy holds that humans are occupational beings and that engaging in meaningful occupation supports health. Core values include altruism, equality, freedom, justice, dignity, truth, and prudence (from the AOTA Code of Ethics). Practice is client-centered and holistic.", ["Humans are occupational beings", "Occupation supports health", "Client-centered, holistic practice", "Core values from the AOTA Code of Ethics"]),
    ch(4, "Current Trends in Occupational Therapy", "Trends include evidence-based practice, occupation-based intervention, telehealth, population health, community-based practice, increased doctoral education, and responding to an aging population and health policy changes.", ["Evidence-based practice", "Telehealth and community practice", "Aging population", "Occupation-based intervention"]),
    ch(5, "A Global Perspective of Occupational Therapy", "OT is practiced worldwide, guided by the World Federation of Occupational Therapists (WFOT). Culture shapes occupations, and occupational justice means all people should have the opportunity to engage in meaningful occupations.", ["WFOT = world federation", "Culture shapes occupation", "Occupational justice", "Global practice"]),
    ch(6, "From Student to Practitioner: Preparation and Certification", "OT education includes academic coursework and fieldwork: Level I (observation and introduction) and Level II (in-depth supervised practice). Graduates take the NBCOT exam, obtain state licensure, and maintain competence through continuing education. Programs are accredited by ACOTE.", ["Level I and Level II fieldwork", "ACOTE accredits programs", "NBCOT exam and state license", "Continuing competence"]),
    ch(7, "Occupational Therapy Roles", "OT practitioners can serve as clinicians, educators, fieldwork educators, supervisors, administrators, researchers, consultants, entrepreneurs, and advocates. OTs are responsible for evaluation and the intervention plan; OTAs carry out interventions under supervision and contribute to evaluation.", ["OT leads evaluation and plan", "OTA implements interventions", "Many career roles", "Supervision levels vary"]),
    ch(8, "Practicing Legally and Ethically", "Legal practice follows state licensure laws and scope of practice. The AOTA Code of Ethics guides behavior with principles such as beneficence, nonmaleficence, autonomy, justice, veracity, and fidelity. Practitioners protect confidentiality (HIPAA) and report unethical behavior.", ["State licensure and scope of practice", "AOTA Code of Ethics", "Beneficence, nonmaleficence, autonomy, justice", "Protect confidentiality"]),
    ch(9, "Leadership and Advocacy", "OT practitioners advocate for clients and the profession by influencing policy, educating the public, and participating in professional organizations such as AOTA and state associations. Leadership skills help practitioners improve services.", ["Advocate for clients and the profession", "AOTA = American Occupational Therapy Association", "Influence policy", "Leadership improves services"]),
    ch(10, "Prioritizing Self-Care", "Practitioners face stress and burnout. Self-care strategies, such as balancing work and personal occupations, setting boundaries, exercise, and mindfulness, support well-being and quality care.", ["Prevent burnout", "Balance occupations", "Set boundaries", "Self-care supports patient care"]),
    ch(11, "The Lived Experience of Occupational Therapy Practitioners", "Practitioners share experiences of working in different settings, building therapeutic relationships, and finding meaning in helping clients regain independence.", ["Diverse practice settings", "Meaning in helping clients", "Learn from practitioners' experience", "Therapeutic relationships matter"]),
    ch(12, "Occupational Therapy Practice Framework: Domain and Process", "The Occupational Therapy Practice Framework (OTPF-4) describes OT's domain: occupations (activities of daily living, instrumental activities of daily living, health management, rest and sleep, education, work, play, leisure, and social participation), contexts, performance patterns, performance skills, and client factors. The process includes evaluation, intervention, and outcomes.", ["OTPF-4 defines domain and process", "ADLs vs. IADLs", "Performance skills and patterns", "Evaluation, intervention, outcomes"]),
    ch(13, "Occupational Therapy Across the Life Span", "OT serves infants and children (development, sensory processing, school function), adolescents, adults (work, injury rehabilitation, mental health), and older adults (fall prevention, aging in place, dementia care). Interventions match each life stage's occupations.", ["Children: play and school", "Adults: work and rehabilitation", "Older adults: aging in place", "Match interventions to life stage"]),
    ch(14, "Treatment Settings and Models of Health Care", "Settings include hospitals, inpatient rehabilitation, skilled nursing facilities, outpatient clinics, schools, home health, mental health programs, and community programs. Models of care range from the medical model to community-based and population health approaches.", ["Hospitals, schools, home health", "Skilled nursing and rehab", "Community-based practice", "Medical vs. community models"]),
    ch(15, "Technology to Support Occupational Engagement", "Technology supports participation: assistive technology (adaptive utensils, reachers, wheelchairs), environmental controls, computer access, augmentative communication devices, and telehealth.", ["Assistive technology", "Adaptive equipment like reachers", "Environmental controls", "Telehealth"]),
    ch(16, "Occupational Therapy Process: Evaluation, Intervention, and Outcomes", "Evaluation begins with an occupational profile (the client's history, interests, and goals) and an analysis of occupational performance. Intervention plans have measurable goals and are implemented and reviewed. Outcomes measure participation, performance, and quality of life. Discontinuation and discharge planning are part of the process.", ["Occupational profile first", "Measurable goals", "Review and adjust interventions", "Measure outcomes"]),
    ch(17, "Models of Practice and Frames of Reference", "Models of practice, such as the Model of Human Occupation (MOHO) and the Person-Environment-Occupation (PEO) model, explain how occupation works. Frames of reference, such as biomechanical, rehabilitative, motor control, sensory integration, and cognitive-behavioral, guide specific interventions.", ["MOHO and PEO models", "Frames of reference guide interventions", "Biomechanical and rehabilitative", "Sensory integration"]),
    ch(18, "Intervention Modalities", "Intervention approaches include occupations and activities, preparatory methods and tasks (such as splinting, exercise, and physical agent modalities), education and training, advocacy, and group interventions. Activity analysis breaks tasks into their demands.", ["Occupations and activities", "Preparatory methods like splints", "Education and training", "Activity analysis"]),
    ch(19, "Therapeutic Relationships", "The therapeutic use of self means practitioners use their personality, insights, and judgment to build trust and motivate clients. Empathy, communication, and collaboration are essential.", ["Therapeutic use of self", "Empathy and trust", "Collaborate with clients", "Communication skills"]),
    ch(20, "Therapeutic Reasoning", "Therapeutic (clinical) reasoning includes scientific, narrative, pragmatic, ethical, interactive, and conditional reasoning. Practitioners use reasoning to understand clients and make decisions throughout the OT process.", ["Scientific and narrative reasoning", "Pragmatic and ethical reasoning", "Interactive and conditional reasoning", "Guides decisions"]),
]

